-- Q3262: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The arrangement is always a service contract since the software runs on the vendor''s servers and the contingent clause is not relevant","Any CCA delivered via internet access is automatically a service contract because the vendor controls the hosting environment","The arrangement qualifies as a software license only if Danforth currently runs the software on its own servers, not based on future rights","The arrangement likely contains a software license because Danforth has a contractual right to take possession and can feasibly run it independently"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASU 2018-15, a CCA contains a software license if two conditions are met: (1) the customer has the contractual right to take possession without significant penalty, and (2) it is feasible for the customer to run the software independently. Danforth''s take-possession clause satisfies the first condition, and its technical capability satisfies the second. Wrong (A): Current hosting location does not determine classification — the contractual right to take possession is what matters. Wrong (B): Internet delivery alone does not make an arrangement a service contract; the two-condition test under ASU 2018-15 governs classification. Wrong (C): ASU 2018-15 evaluates the right and feasibility to take possession, not whether the customer currently operates the software on its own servers.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3262;

-- Q3263: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Are always expensed regardless of the project stage or asset qualification","Are never applicable to internal-use software development projects under GAAP","May be capitalized if the software qualifies as an asset under ASC 835-20 interest capitalization guidance","Are capitalized only when the developing entity is a publicly traded company"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 835-20, interest costs incurred during the development of qualifying assets may be capitalized. Internal-use software under active development in the application stage can qualify if the development period is substantial. The capitalized interest becomes part of the software cost and is amortized over the useful life. Wrong (A): Interest costs are not always expensed — ASC 835-20 permits capitalization for qualifying assets during their development period. Wrong (B): Software projects can qualify for interest capitalization under ASC 835-20 just like other qualifying assets. Wrong (D): Interest capitalization under ASC 835-20 applies to all entities, not just public companies.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3263;

-- Q3264: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Write off the $150,000 remaining book value since the old software is no longer in use","Continue amortizing the old software over its original remaining useful life","Transfer the $150,000 book value to the new cloud computing prepaid asset account","Reclassify the $150,000 as a deferred charge to amortize over the SaaS term"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): When a company abandons on-premises software in favor of a cloud solution, the remaining unamortized book value must be written off as a loss in the period of abandonment under ASC 350-40. The old asset provides no future economic benefit once it is no longer in use. Wrong (B): Continued amortization is inappropriate because the asset has been abandoned and provides no future benefit. Wrong (C): The old software cost cannot be transferred to the new SaaS arrangement — they are separate transactions accounted for independently under ASU 2018-15. Wrong (D): A deferred charge classification has no basis when the underlying asset has been abandoned and generates no future benefit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3264;

-- Q3265: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Is developed primarily for sale or licensing to external customers","Is developed or obtained for internal needs with no plan to market it externally","Is hosted exclusively in a cloud-based computing environment","Must be purchased from a third-party vendor rather than developed in-house"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 350-40, internal-use software is software acquired, internally developed, or modified solely to meet the entity''s internal needs with no substantive plan to market it externally. If the software is marketed externally, ASC 985-20 applies instead. Wrong (A): Software sold to external customers falls under ASC 985-20, not the internal-use software guidance in ASC 350-40. Wrong (C): The delivery method (cloud vs. on-premises) does not define internal-use software — the defining criterion is the intended use. Wrong (D): Internal-use software can be built in-house, purchased, or obtained through a license — there is no requirement to acquire from a third party.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3265;

-- Q3267: Internal-Use Software and Cloud Computing [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Both the license cost and the maintenance fee are expensed immediately as incurred","Both the license cost and the maintenance fee are capitalized as intangible assets","The $300,000 license is capitalized as an intangible asset; the $80,000 maintenance is expensed","The $300,000 license is expensed immediately and the $80,000 maintenance is capitalized"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 350-40, the $300,000 perpetual license cost is capitalized as an intangible asset and amortized over its estimated useful life. The $80,000 annual maintenance fee is a post-implementation cost that does not add significant new functionality and is expensed as incurred. Wrong (A): Perpetual license costs meet the criteria for capitalization and are not expensed immediately. Wrong (B): Routine maintenance fees do not qualify for capitalization because they do not add significant new functionality to the software. Wrong (D): This reverses the correct treatment — the license is the capitalizable item, not the maintenance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3267;

-- Q3268: Advanced Lease Transactions [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The leaseback is classified as an operating lease rather than a finance lease","The transfer of the asset qualifies as a sale under ASC 606 revenue recognition criteria","The lease term is less than 75 percent of the asset''s estimated remaining useful life","The seller-lessee pays cash consideration to exercise a future buyback option"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842, a sale-leaseback is recognized as a sale only if the transfer meets the definition of a sale under ASC 606, meaning control of the asset has transferred to the buyer-lessor. If control has not transferred (e.g., due to a repurchase option), the transaction is a financing arrangement. Wrong (A): Leaseback classification as operating or finance does not determine whether the initial transfer qualifies as a sale. Wrong (C): The 75% threshold was part of legacy lease classification criteria and does not govern sale-leaseback recognition under ASC 842. Wrong (D): The existence or payment of a buyback option would actually prevent sale recognition, not support it.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3268;

-- Q3269: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The full gain or loss on the sale of the factory building immediately in its entirety","Only a gain on the transaction — any losses on sale-leasebacks must be deferred","A gain or loss only to the extent not related to the right-of-use asset retained","No gain or loss because the transaction is recorded at the asset''s carrying value"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under ASC 842, when a sale-leaseback qualifies as a sale, the seller-lessee recognizes gain or loss only on the portion of the asset effectively transferred to the buyer-lessor. The gain related to the right-of-use asset retained through the leaseback is excluded from immediate recognition. Wrong (A): Full immediate recognition is incorrect because the seller-lessee retains a portion of the asset through the leaseback, requiring proportional adjustment. Wrong (B): ASC 842 does not require deferral of losses — both gains and losses are subject to the same proportional recognition approach. Wrong (D): The transaction is recorded at the sale price, not carrying value, and any difference generates a gain or loss subject to the retained-right adjustment.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3269;

-- Q3270: Advanced Lease Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A separate contract if the additional right is priced at its standalone price","Always a modification of the existing lease requiring full remeasurement","A termination of the original lease followed by execution of a new lease","An impairment event requiring reassessment of the existing ROU asset"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 842, a lease modification adding a new right-of-use asset is treated as a separate contract if two conditions are met: the modification increases the scope of the lease, and the consideration is commensurate with the standalone price of the additional right. Wrong (B): Not all modifications require remeasurement of the existing lease — when the separate-contract criteria are met, the original lease is unaffected. Wrong (C): ASC 842 does not require termination and replacement; modifications are either treated as separate contracts or as adjustments to the existing lease. Wrong (D): Adding a new right-of-use asset is a scope change, not an indicator of impairment on the existing asset.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3270;

-- Q3272: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A partial termination with proportional reduction of the ROU asset and lease liability","An adjustment limited to updating the discount rate on the existing lease","A new lease that fully replaces the original lease from the modification date","No accounting change is recorded until the modification formally takes effect"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 842, a modification that decreases the scope of a lease (such as returning leased space) is accounted for as a partial termination. The lessee proportionally reduces the ROU asset and lease liability, and recognizes any difference as a gain or loss in the period of modification. Wrong (B): A partial termination requires proportional adjustment to both the ROU asset and lease liability, not merely a discount rate update. Wrong (C): Reducing scope does not create a new replacement lease — it is a partial termination of the existing lease with proportional adjustments. Wrong (D): ASC 842 requires accounting for the modification when the terms are agreed upon, not deferred until a future effective date.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3272;

-- Q3274: Advanced Lease Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The original head lease classification assigned by the lessor to the lessee","The sublease terms evaluated against the right-of-use asset, not the underlying asset","The sublease must always be classified as an operating lease by the sublessor","The credit quality and financial condition of the sublessee under the agreement"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 842, the sublessor classifies the sublease by reference to the right-of-use asset arising from the head lease, not the underlying asset itself. The standard lease classification criteria in ASC 842-10-25 are applied to the ROU asset as the relevant asset. Wrong (A): The head lease classification does not automatically carry over — the sublease is independently evaluated against the ROU asset. Wrong (C): Subleases are not automatically operating leases; they are classified using the standard criteria applied to the ROU asset. Wrong (D): The sublessee''s credit quality may affect other considerations but does not determine lease classification under ASC 842.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3274;

-- Q3277: Advanced Lease Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A failed sale — the seller-lessee retains the asset and records a financing obligation","An operating lease between the seller-lessee and the buyer-lessor","A capital lease with full gain recognition on the date of the transaction","An off-balance-sheet arrangement with no impact on the financial statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 842, when a sale-leaseback fails the sale criteria (e.g., a repurchase option prevents transfer of control under ASC 606), the asset remains on the seller-lessee''s books and the proceeds are recorded as a financing obligation. No sale, gain, or leaseback is recognized. Wrong (B): Because the transaction fails as a sale, no leaseback is established — it is treated as a financing arrangement, not an operating lease. Wrong (C): No gain can be recognized because the transaction does not qualify as a sale, and capital lease terminology was replaced under ASC 842. Wrong (D): The financing obligation must be recorded on the balance sheet — it is not an off-balance-sheet arrangement.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3277;

-- Q3282: Advanced Lease Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Recognized as rental revenue by the lessee over the lease term","Expensed by the lessee as a leasehold improvement cost when incurred","Added to the initial measurement of the lessee''s lease liability","Used to reduce the measurement of the lessee''s right-of-use asset"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 842-20-30-5, lease incentives received by the lessee — such as tenant improvement allowances — reduce the measurement of the right-of-use asset. This treatment reflects that the incentive lowers the lessee''s effective cost of the lease, resulting in lower amortization expense over the term. Wrong (A): Lease incentives are not revenue — they are part of the lease consideration that reduces the ROU asset. Wrong (B): The allowance is not expensed immediately; it is factored into the ROU asset measurement and recognized over the lease term. Wrong (C): Lease incentives reduce the ROU asset, not increase the lease liability — the liability reflects the present value of lease payments owed.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3282;

-- Q3284: Advanced Lease Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Included in the initial lease liability measurement along with fixed payments","Deferred and recognized as a lump-sum expense at the end of each fiscal year","Estimated at lease commencement and capitalized as part of the ROU asset","Excluded from the lease liability and expensed when the triggering event occurs"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 842-10-15-37, variable lease payments that depend on sales or usage (such as percentage rent tied to gross sales exceeding a threshold) are excluded from the lease liability and ROU asset. They are expensed in the period the triggering event occurs. Wrong (A): Only fixed payments and variable payments tied to an index or rate are included in the initial lease liability — sales-based contingent rent is excluded. Wrong (B): Variable lease payments are recognized in the period when the triggering sales threshold is exceeded, not deferred to year-end. Wrong (C): Sales-contingent payments cannot be reliably estimated at commencement and are not capitalized into the ROU asset under ASC 842.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3284;

-- Q3285: Advanced Lease Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Always the risk-free rate regardless of entity type or available information","The entity''s weighted average cost of capital applied to all lease obligations","The prime rate published by the Federal Reserve at the lease commencement date","The rate implicit in the lease if determinable; otherwise the incremental borrowing rate"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 842, the lessee must use the rate implicit in the lease if it can be readily determined. If not determinable (which is common), the lessee uses its incremental borrowing rate. Private companies may elect the risk-free rate as a practical expedient. Wrong (A): The risk-free rate is only available as an optional practical expedient for private companies — it is not the default for all entities. Wrong (B): WACC is a blended cost-of-capital measure not prescribed by ASC 842 for discounting lease payments. Wrong (C): The prime rate is a general benchmark rate and is not the discount rate specified under ASC 842 lease measurement guidance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3285;

-- Q3288: Advanced Lease Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Derecognize the existing lease entirely and record a new lease at current terms","Record the extension period as a separate lease independent of the original agreement","Make no accounting change until the original ten-year lease term fully expires","Remeasure the lease liability at revised payments and a revised discount rate, adjusting the ROU asset"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 842, a lease term extension is a modification. The lessee remeasures the lease liability using the remaining payments (including the extension) discounted at a revised rate as of the modification date. The ROU asset is adjusted by the same amount as the change in the lease liability. Wrong (A): ASC 842 does not require derecognition and replacement — modifications are accounted for by remeasuring the existing lease. Wrong (B): A term extension is not a separate contract because it does not grant an additional right-of-use asset at standalone price. Wrong (C): The modification must be recognized when the terms are agreed upon, not deferred until the original term expires.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3288;

-- Q3295: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The contribution reduces both the funded status liability and pension expense by $1,000,000 equally","The contribution increases plan assets to $9,500,000, reducing the funded status liability to $500,000 on the balance sheet","The contribution has no balance sheet effect because employer contributions are expensed immediately as pension cost","The contribution reduces plan assets because cash leaves the employer''s control and enters the trust fund"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715, the $1,000,000 employer contribution increases plan assets from $8,500,000 to $9,500,000, reducing the funded status liability from $1,500,000 to $500,000. Pension expense is unaffected because its components (service cost, interest cost, expected return, amortizations) are determined by actuarial assumptions, not cash contributions. Wrong (A): Contributions reduce the funded status liability but do not reduce pension expense — expense is calculated independently of funding decisions. Wrong (C): Contributions increase plan assets and reduce the net liability on the balance sheet; they are not expensed directly. Wrong (D): This reverses the effect — contributions increase plan assets, they do not reduce them.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3295;

-- Q3297: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["10% of the greater of the PBO or the market-related value of plan assets","5% of the projected benefit obligation at the beginning of the period","10% of the lesser of the PBO or the fair value of plan assets","Any cumulative unrecognized amount regardless of size must be amortized immediately"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-30-35-24, the corridor is defined as 10% of the greater of the beginning PBO or the market-related value of plan assets. Only cumulative unrecognized net gains or losses exceeding this corridor are subject to minimum amortization over the average remaining service period. Wrong (B): The threshold is 10%, not 5%, and it is based on the greater of two measures, not the PBO alone. Wrong (C): The corridor uses the greater of the PBO or plan assets, not the lesser — this would set the threshold too low. Wrong (D): Amounts within the corridor are not required to be amortized; the corridor provides a buffer against annual actuarial volatility.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3297;

-- Q3299: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The expected long-term rate of return on the pension plan''s invested assets","The company''s cost of equity capital derived from its stock price and dividends","The risk-free Treasury rate matching the duration of the benefit payments","The rates at which pension benefits could be effectively settled using high-quality bonds"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 715-30-35-43, the discount rate for measuring the PBO should reflect the rates at which pension benefits could be effectively settled. In practice, this means yields on high-quality corporate bonds (typically AA-rated) with durations matching the expected timing of benefit payments. Wrong (A): The expected return on plan assets measures anticipated investment performance, which is a separate assumption from the discount rate used to value the obligation. Wrong (B): The company''s cost of equity is an entity-specific capital market measure unrelated to the settlement rate for pension obligations. Wrong (C): While risk-free rates reflect the time value of money, ASC 715 specifies high-quality fixed-income rates, not Treasury rates, for discounting pension obligations.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3299;

-- Q3301: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["On a pay-as-you-go cash basis, recognizing cost only when benefits are paid to retirees","Similarly to pensions — accrued over service periods using the accumulated postretirement benefit obligation","Only when individual retirees submit claims and the amounts become payable by the employer","As contingent liabilities disclosed in the footnotes but not recognized on the balance sheet"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASC 715-60, other postretirement employee benefits are accounted for on an accrual basis, similar to pension accounting. The employer accrues the cost over employees'' service periods using the accumulated postretirement benefit obligation (APBO) rather than the PBO used for pensions. Wrong (A): Pay-as-you-go accounting was the pre-SFAS 106 practice; ASC 715-60 requires accrual over the service period, not recognition only upon payment. Wrong (C): Waiting for claims would understate the obligation — the cost must be recognized during the periods employees earn the benefits. Wrong (D): OPEB obligations are recognized liabilities on the balance sheet, not merely contingent liabilities disclosed in footnotes.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3301;

-- Q3302: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [hard/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The EPBO is the total expected cost for all postretirement benefits; the APBO is the portion attributed to service rendered to date","The APBO includes more projected future service years than the EPBO covers in its measurement","The EPBO and APBO are identical measures that differ only in their disclosure presentation","The EPBO is used exclusively for pension plans and does not apply to retiree health care"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under ASC 715-60-20, the EPBO represents the total expected cost of all postretirement benefits for an employee over their full retirement period. The APBO is the portion of the EPBO attributed to service rendered to date, prorated based on service to date versus total expected service to the full eligibility date. At full eligibility, APBO equals EPBO. Wrong (B): The APBO is always less than or equal to the EPBO because it reflects only service rendered to date, not additional future service. Wrong (C): They are distinct measures — the EPBO is the total obligation and the APBO is the earned portion allocated to past service. Wrong (D): The EPBO is defined under ASC 715-60 specifically for postretirement benefits other than pensions, not for pension plans.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3302;

-- Q3307: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["As a component of other income and expense below the operating income line","Within the same line items as other employee compensation costs in operating expense","As a separate line item presented below operating income on the income statement","In other comprehensive income along with actuarial gains and losses for the period"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under ASU 2017-07, service cost is the only component of net periodic pension cost that must be reported within operating expense, in the same line items as other employee compensation costs. All other components (interest cost, expected return, amortizations) are reported outside operating income. Wrong (A): Service cost is an operating expense, not an other income/expense item — it represents the cost of benefits earned by employees during the current period. Wrong (C): ASU 2017-07 requires service cost to be presented with employee compensation, not as a separate line below operating income. Wrong (D): Service cost is recognized in net income, not in other comprehensive income — OCI treatment applies to certain actuarial adjustments, not to service cost.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3307;

-- Q3309: Pension and Postretirement Benefits [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Decrease the APBO because higher trend rates reduce the present value of future benefits","Decrease service cost only while leaving the APBO and interest cost components unchanged","Have no effect on OPEB measurements since trend rates apply only to pension plans","Increase both the APBO and the net periodic postretirement benefit cost for the plan"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under ASC 715-60, a higher health care cost trend rate increases projected future health care costs, which increases the APBO (the obligation) and the service cost and interest cost components of net periodic postretirement benefit cost. The trend rate is among the most sensitive OPEB assumptions. Wrong (A): Higher trend rates increase, not decrease, the APBO because they project greater future health care costs. Wrong (B): The effect is not limited to service cost — the APBO, interest cost, and service cost are all affected by the trend rate change. Wrong (C): The health care cost trend rate is specific to OPEB and directly affects OPEB measurements under ASC 715-60.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3309;

-- Q3310: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["GASB requires dual reporting because federal law mandates it for all governmental entities regardless of size","Fund statements demonstrate fiscal accountability using modified accrual; government-wide statements demonstrate operational accountability using full accrual","The reconciliation exists solely to identify accounting errors and should show zero differences if prepared correctly","GASB requires two sets only because government-wide statements cannot present the fund-level detail needed by stakeholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): GASB 34 established the dual-perspective reporting model based on two distinct accountability objectives. Fund statements use the current financial resources measurement focus and modified accrual basis to demonstrate fiscal accountability, while government-wide statements use the economic resources measurement focus and full accrual basis to demonstrate operational accountability. Wrong (A): The dual reporting requirement comes from GASB standards, not federal law — it is a GAAP requirement for state and local governments. Wrong (C): The reconciliation bridges systematic differences between measurement focuses, not accounting errors between the two statement sets. Wrong (D): The difference is conceptual (measurement focus and basis of accounting), not merely a matter of presentation detail versus summary.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3310;

-- Q3311: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["They are subtracted from governmental activities in the reconciliation adjustment","No adjustment is needed because capital outlays are treated the same on both statements","They are added back because they are capitalized, not expensed, on government-wide statements","They are reclassified from expenditures to revenue on the government-wide statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 34 (paragraphs 77-78), capital outlays recorded as expenditures in governmental funds must be added back in the reconciliation because government-wide statements capitalize these amounts as assets under the economic resources measurement focus. The assets are then depreciated over their useful lives. Wrong (A): Capital outlays are added back, not subtracted — they represent asset acquisitions, not reductions. Wrong (B): An adjustment is required because governmental funds expense capital outlays while government-wide statements capitalize them. Wrong (D): Capital outlays are reclassified as capital assets, not revenue — they represent resource acquisitions, not inflows.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3311;

-- Q3312: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Governmental funds do not own or hold title to any capital assets under GASB standards","Depreciation expense is optional for all governmental entities under current GASB guidance","Governmental funds use the current financial resources focus, which excludes long-term assets and depreciation","Capital assets and their depreciation are reported exclusively in fiduciary fund statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under GASB 34 (paragraphs 18-20), governmental funds use the current financial resources measurement focus, which reports only current assets and liabilities. Capital assets are not recognized in governmental funds, so depreciation does not appear. Government-wide statements use the economic resources focus and report depreciation. Wrong (A): Governmental funds do own capital assets, but the current financial resources focus does not report them on fund statements. Wrong (B): Depreciation is required, not optional, for capital assets reported on government-wide statements under GASB 34. Wrong (D): Fiduciary funds report assets held in trust for others, not the government''s own capital assets and infrastructure.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3312;

-- Q3313: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Left as revenue on the government-wide statements without any reconciliation adjustment","Removed because they represent a long-term liability, not revenue, on government-wide statements","Added directly to net position as an increase in the government''s overall equity balance","Reclassified from other financing sources to investment income on government-wide statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34 (paragraph 77), bond proceeds reported as other financing sources in governmental funds must be reclassified as a long-term liability on the government-wide statements. Borrowing does not increase net position — it creates an asset (cash) and an offsetting liability simultaneously. Wrong (A): Bond proceeds cannot remain as revenue because they represent debt, not earned resources, under the economic resources measurement focus. Wrong (C): Bond proceeds do not increase net position — the cash inflow is fully offset by the new long-term liability. Wrong (D): Bond proceeds are debt financing, not investment income — they represent borrowed funds that must be repaid.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3313;

-- Q3314: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The principal payment is an expense on both statements because debt repayment always reduces net position equally","The payment has no effect on either statement because bond principal payments are always treated as off-balance-sheet items","The payment reduces fund balance but increases net position because eliminating debt creates additional governmental value","On fund statements it is an expenditure reducing fund balance; on government-wide statements it reduces bonds payable, not expense"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 34, the $5,000,000 principal payment is an expenditure on fund statements (reducing fund balance) but on government-wide statements it reduces the long-term bonds payable liability rather than being an expense. The reconciliation removes the expenditure and records the liability reduction. Net position is unchanged because cash and bonds payable decrease equally. Wrong (A): Debt repayment is not an expense on government-wide statements — it is a balance sheet transaction reducing both cash and the liability. Wrong (B): The payment has significant effects on both statement sets and is not an off-balance-sheet transaction. Wrong (C): Net position is unchanged, not increased — both cash and the liability decrease by the same $5,000,000 amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3314;

-- Q3316: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Adds the amount to revenue because full accrual recognizes it when earned regardless of collection","Subtracts the amount from net position as an adjustment to the government''s overall equity","Leaves it as a deferred inflow on both the fund and government-wide financial statements","Converts the deferred inflow into a long-term liability on the government-wide statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under GASB 34 (paragraphs 77-79), revenue deferred in governmental funds because it is unavailable (not collected within the availability period) must be recognized as revenue on government-wide statements under full accrual. The reconciliation converts these deferred inflows to revenue, increasing net position. Wrong (B): The adjustment increases revenue and net position — it does not subtract from net position. Wrong (C): The deferred inflow exists only under modified accrual; under full accrual on government-wide statements, the revenue is recognized when earned. Wrong (D): Unavailable revenue is not a liability — it represents earned revenue that has not yet been collected, which is recognized as revenue under full accrual.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3316;

-- Q3317: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Subtracted from governmental activities because internal service funds are proprietary funds","Added to governmental activities because internal service funds predominantly serve governmental functions","Reported exclusively with business-type activities alongside enterprise fund balances","Eliminated entirely so that it does not appear on the government-wide statements"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under GASB 34 (paragraph 62), internal service fund net position is typically consolidated with governmental activities in the government-wide statements because these funds predominantly serve governmental functions such as motor pool, IT, and printing services. Wrong (A): Internal service funds are added to, not subtracted from, governmental activities because their services support governmental programs. Wrong (C): Enterprise funds are reported with business-type activities, but internal service funds are consolidated with governmental activities due to their primarily internal governmental purpose. Wrong (D): Internal service fund balances are not eliminated — they are reclassified and consolidated into governmental activities on the government-wide statements.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3317;

-- Q3319: Fund-to-Government-Wide Reconciliation [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A long-term liability recognized on the governmental fund balance sheet for the full amount","Deferred outflows of resources reported on both fund and government-wide statements","An expense accrued over each employee''s service period on the fund statements","An expenditure only when amounts are due and payable from current available resources"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under GASB 34 (paragraph 80), compensated absences in governmental funds are recognized as expenditures only when they mature — that is, when they become due and payable with currently available resources. Only the $85,000 currently due is recognized as a fund expenditure. Wrong (A): Governmental funds do not report long-term liabilities — the full $1.2 million obligation appears only on the government-wide statements under full accrual. Wrong (B): Compensated absences are not classified as deferred outflows of resources — they are liabilities on government-wide statements and expenditures when matured on fund statements. Wrong (C): Governmental funds use modified accrual, which recognizes expenditures when liabilities mature, not accrual-basis expense recognition over the service period.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3319;
